begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-001','[["Project characteristics and temporary endeavour","A department wants to treat its recurring monthly payroll processing as a project and appoint a project manager, even though the work has no defined end and follows an established routine.","Classify the work as operations, and reserve project governance for a temporary effort that creates a unique result.","Create a project charter for every payroll cycle because all organised work is a project.","Declare the payroll function complete after the first month and restart it as a new project each month.","Use only a risk register to distinguish the work from a project.","A project is temporary and produces a unique product, service or result; repetitive ongoing work is operations."],["Clear objectives and success criteria","The sponsor says the project will be successful if it ''improves service delivery'', but no measurable target, deadline or acceptance condition has been agreed.","Translate the aspiration into specific, measurable objectives and documented success criteria agreed with the sponsor.","Begin execution immediately and define success after the deliverables are produced.","Allow each functional manager to use a different definition of success.","Measure success only by whether the approved budget is fully spent.","Measurable objectives and acceptance criteria provide a basis for planning, monitoring and determining whether intended value has been achieved."],["Triple constraint and integrated trade-offs","A client requests an additional reporting feature without extending the deadline or budget, and the team estimates that the change will require significant extra work.","Assess the impact on scope, time, cost, quality and risk, then obtain an authorised trade-off decision through change control.","Add the feature informally and require the team to absorb the extra work.","Reject every change because a project baseline can never be revised.","Reduce testing without informing stakeholders so the original deadline is preserved.","Changes to one constraint usually affect others; an integrated impact assessment and authorised decision are required."],["Project life cycle and tailoring","A project team copies the same life-cycle phases and controls used on a large highway project for a small two-week internal process improvement initiative.","Tailor the life cycle, governance and documentation to the project''s size, risk, complexity and delivery context.","Use the full highway governance model because every project must have identical controls.","Remove all planning and oversight because the initiative is short.","Select project phases only according to the personal preference of the project manager.","Project management should be tailored so controls are proportionate while still protecting objectives, accountability and quality."],["Stakeholder identification and engagement","A technically sound project is delayed after a host community blocks site access because community leaders were not identified or consulted during planning.","Update the stakeholder register, analyse influence and interests, and implement a targeted engagement plan with the affected community.","Continue work with security support and avoid further dialogue.","Communicate only with the sponsor because the community is not funding the project.","Wait until project closure before documenting the community concern.","Early stakeholder identification, analysis and appropriate engagement reduce resistance and improve legitimacy and delivery."],["Project manager role and competencies","Functional specialists are working in silos, decisions are delayed and no one has a shared view of priorities, although each specialist is technically competent.","Integrate the work, clarify responsibilities, facilitate decisions and communicate a shared delivery direction.","Perform every technical task personally to remove dependence on specialists.","Allow each function to optimise its own outputs without coordinating dependencies.","Escalate all routine decisions to the sponsor rather than leading the team.","The project manager integrates people, processes and information, provides leadership and coordinates decisions across functions."],["Work Breakdown Structure and the 100 percent rule","A project schedule omits training, handover and regulatory approval because the team decomposed only the physical construction deliverables.","Revise the WBS so it captures 100 percent of the approved project scope, including product and project-management work.","Keep the WBS unchanged and add omitted work only when invoices are submitted.","List only major milestones because work packages are unnecessary.","Move the omitted activities into the risk register instead of the WBS.","The WBS should represent the full approved scope; omitted deliverables and management work create planning and control gaps."],["Scheduling, dependencies and critical path","The team wants to shorten a delayed project but has not identified which activities control the completion date.","Develop or update the network schedule, calculate the critical path and focus acceleration decisions on critical activities.","Shorten the duration of any activity with the largest budget.","Add resources equally to every activity, including those with substantial float.","Move the final milestone earlier without changing activity logic or durations.","Critical-path analysis identifies activities with zero or least float that determine the project completion date."],["Resource allocation and resource levelling","Two critical activities require the same specialist during the same week, but the specialist cannot work on both simultaneously.","Resolve the over-allocation by levelling or reallocating the resource while assessing the effect on the critical path and completion date.","Assign the specialist to both activities and assume productivity will double.","Remove the resource requirement from the schedule without changing the work.","Delay a nonrelated milestone without analysing the resource conflict.","Resource levelling adjusts timing or assignments to resolve over-allocation and must be assessed against schedule consequences."],["Cost estimation and budgeting","A budget was prepared by copying last year''s total cost, although scope, quantities, inflation and exchange-rate exposure have changed.","Prepare a basis-of-estimate using current scope, quantities, rates, assumptions, risks and appropriate contingency.","Use the old total because historical figures are always more reliable than current analysis.","Exclude contingency so the budget appears more competitive.","Estimate only direct labour and treat all other costs as future changes.","A defensible budget links estimates to the current scope, assumptions, market conditions and identified uncertainty."],["Project management software","The team believes installing scheduling software will automatically produce a realistic plan even though activities, dependencies and estimates have not been validated.","First develop and validate the planning logic and data, then use software to calculate, visualise and control the approved schedule.","Allow the software to invent missing dependencies and resource assumptions.","Use the most complex software available regardless of team capability.","Replace stakeholder review with automated reports generated by the tool.","Software supports project management; it does not replace sound scope definition, estimating, logic, governance or professional judgement."],["Monitoring, control and authorised change","Actual progress is behind the baseline, but the team changes dates in the schedule each week so reports continue to show the project as on time.","Preserve the approved baseline, report the variance, analyse causes and forecast impact, then process any justified baseline change formally.","Continue replacing baseline dates with actual dates to eliminate variance.","Stop reporting until performance returns to plan.","Record the delay only in meeting minutes and take no corrective action.","Control requires comparison with an authorised baseline, transparent variance analysis, forecasting and controlled corrective or change action."],["Risk management process","The team records only problems that have already happened and calls the list a risk register.","Separate current issues from uncertain future events, then identify, analyse, assign and plan responses for risks before they occur.","Keep only realised problems because future uncertainty cannot be managed.","Transfer every risk to the sponsor without analysis.","Delete low-probability risks even when their potential impact is severe.","Risks are uncertain future events or conditions; issues have occurred. Proactive identification and response planning are essential."],["Project quality management","A contractor proposes relying entirely on final inspection, although defects are repeatedly introduced by an unstable work process.","Define quality requirements, improve and assure the process, and use control activities to verify outputs throughout delivery.","Accept defects until final inspection because quality is tested only at completion.","Increase production volume so defective units become a smaller percentage.","Leave quality responsibility solely to the client at handover.","Quality should be planned and built into processes, then controlled through measurement and verification rather than inspected in only at the end."],["Project closure and lessons learned","The deliverables are in use, but acceptance, contract closure, archived records and lessons learned have not been completed.","Conduct formal closure, obtain acceptance, close obligations, transfer ownership, archive records and capture lessons for future projects.","Disband the team immediately because operational use proves formal closure is unnecessary.","Leave open contracts for the operations team to resolve informally.","Delete project records after the final payment to reduce storage costs.","Formal closure confirms acceptance, completes obligations, preserves organisational knowledge and transfers responsibility appropriately."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
